sections:
  - eyebrow: "The Challenge"
    heading: "Fragmentation Is the Root Problem"
    body: "Healthcare information is scattered across hospitals, clinics, laboratories, pharmacies, insurers, and research institutions, each storing and structuring knowledge differently. The result is not a shortage of data — it is a shortage of connection. Clinicians work across isolated systems, patients move between disconnected providers, and discoveries that could change outcomes often take years to reach the people who need them. Most healthcare systems remain reactive by design, intervening after a problem has already developed rather than helping people and professionals anticipate it earlier."
  - eyebrow: "Our Perspective"
    heading: "Intelligence Should Connect, Not Just Compute"
    body: "Adding more software to a fragmented system does not solve fragmentation — it can deepen it. The technologies that matter most are the ones designed from the outset to connect people, knowledge, and care into a single, trustworthy ecosystem, rather than solving one narrow problem in isolation. That is a systems question as much as a technical one: it requires interoperability, shared standards, and a willingness to design for the whole healthcare journey instead of a single feature."
